/* data/detail-c/mth-11-10.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/mth-11-10.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-11-10"] = {"mth-11-10-m01":{"idea":"Continuity at a point means the graph has no break there. Three things must hold together.","steps":["First, f(a) must be defined.","Second, the limit of f(x) as x tends to a must exist.","Third, that limit must be equal to f(a).","If any one of the three fails, f is discontinuous at a."],"others":"A function not defined at a is discontinuous there. A limit alone is not enough, since f(a) may differ from it. Having f(a) defined is not enough either, because the limit may not exist.","trap":"Students check only the limit, or only f(a). You need all three.","answer":"f(a) is defined, the limit as x tends to a exists, and the limit equals f(a)"},"mth-11-10-m02":{"idea":"Compare the limit with the stored value f(2). If they differ, the function has a break.","steps":["For x ≠ 2, (x² − 4)/(x − 2) = (x − 2)(x + 2)/(x − 2) = x + 2.","So the limit as x tends to 2 is 2 + 2 = 4.","But f(2) is given as 3.","Limit 4 is not equal to f(2) = 3, so f is discontinuous at x = 2."],"others":"Being defined at 2 is not enough, the value must match the limit. The limit is 4, not 3. The limit does exist, so it is not a case of no limit.","trap":"Do not stop at 'f(2) is defined'. Always compare it with the limit.","answer":"discontinuous at x = 2, since the limit is 4 but f(2) = 3"},"mth-11-10-m03":{"idea":"To remove a hole in a graph, define the missing value to be the limit.","steps":["For x ≠ 2, (x² − 4)/(x − 2) = x + 2.","The limit as x tends to 2 is 2 + 2 = 4.","For continuity, f(2) must equal this limit.","So f(2) = 4."],"others":"Saying it does not exist is wrong, because the limit exists and equals 4. The values 2 and 0 do not match the limit, so a break would remain.","trap":"Putting x = 2 directly gives 0/0. Cancel the factor first.","answer":"4"},"mth-11-10-m04":{"idea":"A jump discontinuity is a sudden change of level: both one-sided limits exist, but they are not equal.","steps":["Let the left-hand limit be L and the right-hand limit be R.","In a jump, both L and R are finite numbers.","But L ≠ R, so the graph jumps.","Example: [x] at x = 2 has L = 1 and R = 2."],"others":"A limit growing without bound is an infinite discontinuity. A limit equal to f(a) means continuity. A function undefined on both sides of a has no limit question at all.","answer":"the left-hand and the right-hand limits exist but are different"},"mth-11-10-m05":{"idea":"In an infinite discontinuity the values blow up near the point, like 1/x near 0.","steps":["Take f(x) = 1/x near x = 0.","As x gets close to 0, |f(x)| gets larger and larger.","The graph has a vertical asymptote at x = 0.","This growing without bound is called an infinite discontinuity."],"others":"The same finite limit on both sides gives a removable break or continuity. Two different finite limits give a jump. A continuous function has no break at all.","answer":"the function grows without bound as x tends to a"},"mth-11-10-m06":{"idea":"A rational function breaks where its denominator is zero. So factorise the denominator.","steps":["Denominator: x² − 5x + 6.","Factorise: x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3).","It is zero when x = 2 or x = 3.","So f is discontinuous exactly at x = 2 and x = 3."],"others":"The pair 5 and 6 are the numbers in the middle term, not the roots. The value 2 alone misses x = 3. The pair −2 and −3 gives (x + 2)(x + 3), a different expression.","trap":"Quick check: put x = 2 in the denominator: 4 − 10 + 6 = 0.","answer":"x = 2 and x = 3"},"mth-11-10-m07":{"idea":"Polynomials are built from sums and products of continuous pieces, so they never break.","steps":["The function f(x) = x and a constant are continuous everywhere.","Sums and products of continuous functions are continuous.","A polynomial is made only from such sums and products.","So a polynomial is continuous at every real number."],"others":"It is not limited to integers, since it is continuous at all real points. There is no break at x = 0. It is continuous on the whole real line, not only on closed intervals.","answer":"they are continuous at every real number"},"mth-11-10-m08":{"idea":"A quotient of continuous functions is continuous wherever the denominator is not zero.","steps":["Division by zero is not allowed.","So f(x)/g(x) is defined only where g(x) ≠ 0.","At those points, the quotient rule for limits applies and gives continuity.","Where g(x) = 0 there may be a break."],"others":"f(x) = g(x) has no role in this. Needing f(x) ≠ 0 is wrong, since a zero numerator is fine. The claim of all points fails at zeros of the denominator.","answer":"at all the points where g(x) is not zero"},"mth-11-10-m09":{"idea":"On an open interval, continuity is checked at inside points only. The end points are not included.","steps":["The interval (a, b) contains points strictly between a and b.","Continuous on (a, b) means continuous at each of these points.","No condition is needed at a or b, since they are not in the interval."],"others":"A constant value is not required. Being defined is not enough if there are breaks. Checking only the end points checks the wrong places.","trap":"Closed interval [a, b] is different: end points need one-sided continuity.","answer":"it is continuous at every point of the interval"},"mth-11-10-m10":{"idea":"At an end point of a closed interval, only one side lies inside the interval. So only a one-sided limit is used.","steps":["At a, the points of [a, b] lie to the right of a.","So we need the right-hand limit at a to equal f(a).","At b, the points lie to the left of b.","So we need the left-hand limit at b to equal f(b)."],"others":"Left at a and right at b would use points outside the interval. Just being defined is not enough. Equal to zero at the ends has nothing to do with continuity.","answer":"continuous from the right at a, and continuous from the left at b"},"mth-11-10-m11":{"idea":"A piece-wise function follows different rules on each side, so check each side separately.","steps":["Find the left-hand limit using the rule on the left.","Find the right-hand limit using the rule on the right.","Find the value of the function at the point.","Continuity needs all three to be equal."],"others":"Using only one rule ignores what happens on the other side. An average of the two rules compared with zero has no meaning for continuity.","answer":"find the left-hand and right-hand limits separately, and compare them with the value of the function"},"mth-11-10-m12":{"idea":"For a piece-wise function, make the left value equal to the right limit at the joining point.","steps":["Left side at x = 2: f(2) = k(2)² = 4k.","Right limit: 3(2) − 2 = 4.","Continuity needs 4k = 4.","So k = 1."],"others":"If k = 2, the left value is 8, not 4. If k = 4, it is 16. If k = 1/2, it is 2. None of these equals 4.","trap":"Quick check: k = 1 gives 4 on the left, and 4 on the right.","answer":"1"},"mth-11-10-m13":{"idea":"Use the standard limit sin u / u → 1 as u → 0, after matching the angle with the denominator.","steps":["(sin 5x)/x = 5 × (sin 5x)/(5x).","As x → 0, 5x → 0, so (sin 5x)/(5x) → 1.","So the limit is 5 × 1 = 5.","For continuity, f(0) = 5."],"others":"The value 0 comes from wrongly using sin 0 only. The value 1/5 divides instead of multiplying. The value 1 forgets the factor 5.","answer":"5"},"mth-11-10-m14":{"idea":"Rewrite 1 − cos x with a half-angle identity so the standard limit sin u / u → 1 can be used.","steps":["1 − cos x = 2 sin²(x/2).","So (1 − cos x)/x² = 2 sin²(x/2)/x².","Write x² = 4 (x/2)². This gives (1/2) × (sin(x/2)/(x/2))².","As x → 0, the bracket tends to 1, so the limit is 1/2.","So f(0) = 1/2."],"others":"The value 1 forgets the factor 1/2 left after writing x² as 4 times (x/2)². The value 2 is the reverse. The value 0 uses cos 0 = 1 only on top.","answer":"1/2"},"mth-11-10-m15":{"idea":"A continuous function cannot skip values. Between f(a) and f(b), it hits every value.","steps":["Let f be continuous on [a, b].","Pick any number k between f(a) and f(b).","Then there is some c in [a, b] with f(c) = k.","The graph has no gaps, so it cannot jump over k."],"others":"Only f(a) and f(b) would be too few values. Zero is only taken if it lies between them. A value larger than both is not promised.","answer":"every value between f(a) and f(b) at some point of [a, b]"},"mth-11-10-m16":{"idea":"If a continuous function changes sign, it must cross zero in between.","steps":["Let f be continuous on [a, b].","Suppose f(a) < 0 and f(b) > 0, or the other way round.","Zero lies between f(a) and f(b).","So f(c) = 0 for some c in (a, b)."],"others":"Increasing alone does not give a sign change. Same signs at both ends give no guarantee. f(a) = f(b) says nothing about zero.","answer":"f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs"},"mth-11-10-m17":{"idea":"Apply the intermediate value theorem: continuity plus a sign change guarantees a root.","steps":["f(x) = x³ − x − 1 is a polynomial, so it is continuous.","f(1) = 1 − 1 − 1 = −1, which is negative.","f(2) = 8 − 2 − 1 = 5, which is positive.","So there is at least one root in (1, 2)."],"others":"No root contradicts the sign change. Exactly two roots is not promised, the theorem says at least one. Exactly 1.5 is wrong, since f(1.5) = 0.875, which is not 0.","answer":"the equation f(x) = 0 has at least one root between 1 and 2"},"mth-11-10-m18":{"idea":"The theorem needs continuity. A jump can skip values, so the conclusion can fail.","steps":["For x < 0, f(x) = −1. For x ≥ 0, f(x) = 1.","f takes only the two values −1 and 1.","Zero lies between −1 and 1, but f is never 0.","The jump at x = 0 breaks continuity, so the theorem does not apply."],"others":"The value at x = 0 is 1, not 0. Not every value between −1 and 1 is taken, only two values. At x = −1 the value is −1, not 0, so no root there.","answer":"never takes the value 0, though it goes from −1 to 1"},"mth-11-10-m19":{"idea":"The theorem says a root exists. It does not tell where it is or how many there are.","steps":["It guarantees at least one c with f(c) = 0.","It gives no formula for c.","To find c, use a method such as bisection or Newton's method."],"others":"Increasing is not proved. Exactly one root is not proved, there may be several. The mid-point is a guess, not a result.","trap":"Existence is not the same as uniqueness.","answer":"a root exists in the interval, but it does not give its value"},"mth-11-10-n01":{"idea":"At the joining point, the left value must equal the right limit.","steps":["Left: f(2) = k × 2² = 4k.","Right limit: 3(2) − 2 = 4.","Set them equal: 4k = 4.","So k = 1.000."],"trap":"Quick check: with k = 1, the left side gives 4, matching the right.","answer":"1.000"},"mth-11-10-n02":{"idea":"A hole is filled by defining the value as the limit. Cancel the common factor first.","steps":["(x² − 4)/(x − 2) = (x − 2)(x + 2)/(x − 2) = x + 2 for x ≠ 2.","Limit as x → 2: 2 + 2 = 4.","So L = 4.000."],"trap":"Direct substitution gives 0/0, which means: factorise.","answer":"4.000"},"mth-11-10-n03":{"idea":"Use the standard limit sin u / u → 1 as u → 0.","steps":["(sin 5x)/x = 5 × (sin 5x)/(5x).","As x → 0, (sin 5x)/(5x) → 1.","So the limit is 5 × 1 = 5.","L = 5.000."],"answer":"5.000"},"mth-11-10-n04":{"idea":"Use a half-angle identity to turn 1 − cos x into a sine, then use sin u / u → 1.","steps":["1 − cos x = 2 sin²(x/2).","(1 − cos x)/x² = 2 sin²(x/2)/(4 (x/2)²) = (1/2)(sin(x/2)/(x/2))².","As x → 0, the bracket → 1.","The limit is 1/2, so L = 0.500."],"answer":"0.500"},"mth-11-10-n05":{"idea":"Continuity at both joining points gives two equations in a and b.","steps":["At x = 1: left value is 2, so a(1) + b = 2, i.e. a + b = 2.","At x = 3: right value is 8, so 3a + b = 8.","Subtract the first from the second: 2a = 6.","So a = 3.000. Then b = 2 − 3 = −1."],"trap":"Check: at x = 3, 3(3) − 1 = 8, as needed.","answer":"3.000"},"mth-11-10-n06":{"idea":"A rational function is discontinuous at the zeros of its denominator. Count those inside the interval.","steps":["x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3).","It is zero at x = 2 and x = 3.","Both 2 and 3 lie in [0, 10].","So there are 2 points of discontinuity."],"answer":"2"},"mth-11-10-n07":{"idea":"The intermediate value theorem gives a root, then bisection narrows it down.","steps":["f(x) = x³ − x − 1 has f(1) = −1 and f(2) = 5, a sign change.","Mid-point 1.5: f = 0.875 > 0, so the root is in (1, 1.5).","Mid-point 1.25: f = −0.297 < 0, so the root is in (1.25, 1.5).","Keep halving the interval, always keeping the sign change.","The root settles at 1.3247."],"trap":"Check: 1.3247³ = 2.3247, and 1.3247 + 1 = 2.3247.","answer":"1.3247"},"mth-11-10-n08":{"idea":"Turn cos x = x into f(x) = cos x − x = 0. Then find where the sign changes.","steps":["f(0) = cos 0 − 0 = 1, which is positive.","f(1) = cos 1 − 1 = 0.5403 − 1 = −0.4597, which is negative.","f is continuous, so a root lies in (0, 1).","Halving the interval repeatedly gives x = 0.7391."],"trap":"Check with a calculator in radian mode: cos 0.7391 = 0.7391.","answer":"0.7391"}};
